  "evidence": "TEXT_E: modernise the NHS with faster free treatment; hire 20,000 extra nurses and 10,000 extra doctors; add 6,000 police and reform criminal justice to catch, punish and rehabilitate; stronger support for children and families via tax and benefits; better cash and services for pensioners; raise the minimum wage to £4.20; help seven million adults without basic skills and aim for full employment in every region; engage and lead in Europe with the public having the final say on the euro. TEXT_C: increase NHS spending by £2bn and raise nurses’ pay; hire 30,000 more teachers and 25,000 more police; simplify benefits so work always pays and raise the income‑tax threshold for low earners; leave the EU and restore full sovereignty over laws, borders and trade; adopt skills‑based immigration and deterrent sentencing; cut fuel duty and deregulate to boost small business.",
  "reasoning": "Given the electorate’s highest weights on Care, then Authority and Equality, TEXT_E more squarely targets care and equality (NHS expansion free at point of use, family and pensioner support, minimum wage, regional full employment) while maintaining authority through policing and justice reform. TEXT_C does address care and authority, but it emphasizes national sovereignty/loyalty and proportionality via deregulation and tax cuts, which are lower‑priority in the profile, so TEXT_E aligns more closely overall.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
